= Improvement Opportunities <sec:opportunities>

== Immediate, evidence-backed changes
*Keep the support-signature cache and test its invariants.* Its controlled benefit is representation work, not pruning: @tab:ablation records identical 2,466 assignment nodes with and without the cache and about 14.1% lower total time with it on the selected nine runs. The immediate engineering action is to preserve cache/trail correctness during refactoring. This is the only quantified implementation-overhead improvement isolated by unchanged work counts; it should not be extrapolated to every dictionary or dense instance without repetitions.

*Investigate strategy selection, but do not ship a grid-ID rule.* On the three core 90 seeds the forward-checking/no-arc configuration consumed 0.050 s against MAC's 0.377 s, whereas the Gecode 45 failure favours MAC. An evidence-backed opportunity is to characterize predictors of this trade-off and validate a scheduler on a held-out corpus. The 0.327 s core-90 saving is an observed upper opportunity for those runs, not a promised suite-wide gain. A portfolio must charge both strategies' work to the same deadline; the best-of-two table is not a free algorithm.

*Repeat small-effect measurements before tuning ties.* Removing the degree tie-break changed total time by only about 1.6% in @tab:ablation. The immediate action is additional interleaved runs, not an assertion that the tie-break matters greatly. An isolated comparison can quantify timing variance while checking that node differences are reproducible. Changing sample/restart constants requires a declared train/held-out split; this campaign contains no evidence for an optimal alternative value.

== Established engineering upgrades
*Stronger all-different filtering.* Matching-based pruning can detect a set of slots competing for too few words even when no domain is singleton @regin94. Crossbot currently misses these Hall-like failures. A selective implementation for small heavily constrained same-length domains is an established upgrade; its overhead and activation frequency on this corpus remain unmeasured. No percentage speedup is promised from the literature alone.

*Weighted conflicts and backjumping with precise explanations.* Dom/weighted-degree ordering @boussemart04 and conflict-directed backjumping @prosser93 can concentrate search and skip irrelevant decisions. The coarse prototype logs do not establish that these principles are ineffective; they establish only that those implementations did not solve core 87 within their recorded budgets. A revised experiment must preserve sound explanations through crossing reductions, distinctness deletions and restoration, and compare assignment counts as well as wall time on both families.

*Structure-aware search with distinctness retained.* Graph-based ordering and clustering @freuder82 @dechter87 and crossword-specific partition ideas @ginsberg11 provide established starting points. Core 87's four long linking slots suggest caching conditional region feasibility or explicit separator search. Distinctness couples otherwise disjoint crossing regions, so cached witnesses need an interface for used words or a sound recombination check. The required experiment is an exact, generic decomposition with every subproblem's work charged to the common deadline, not four independently claimed successes.

*More informative candidate guidance.* Belief propagation originates in probabilistic inference @pearl82 and is used in modern clued filling @wallace22. It could account for correlations beyond immediate support products, but implementing and charging its iterations is essential. Bitwise AC-4/AC-6-style support management and Compact-Table-like techniques offer alternative propagation trade-offs @mohr86 @bessiere94 @compact16. These are established upgrades, not evidence that they will improve this particular Python equality specialization.

== Research directions
*Find a witness or a proof for core 87.* The explicit open status is the central outstanding question. A new experiment should include all three supplied dictionaries, multiple seeds, independent witness verification and time-to-first-fill distributions. If a longer-budget solver finds a witness, five-second performance can then be studied without confusing scarcity with implementation failure. If a complete search proves unsatisfiability for one dictionary, the goal still requires investigation of the others. Neither a timeout nor the first-moment proxy is a certificate.

*Measure a real feasibility transition.* The crude surrogate in @sec:evaluation ignores correlations and predicts many assignments even on the unresolved case. A controlled thinning experiment should start from known-fill dense grids, preserve or explicitly remove witness words, vary dictionary fractions and use a decision method capable of resolving enough unsatisfiable cases. Phase-transition and constrainedness methodology @cheeseman91 @gent96 motivates the protocol, but the experiment needs a substantially broader dense-grid corpus before “near boundary” can become a reliable structural label.

*Test generalization and objective changes.* The supplied dictionaries differ in letter morphology, while clued systems operate on weighted candidates @littman99 @ginsberg11. A new corpus should separate variable-length sparse grids from dense square-like blocks and should include locks, decomposed crossing graphs with word-reuse conflicts and Spanish `ñ`. Cold-process indexing time, memory peaks and held-out strategy selection must be measured. This would establish which representation and ranking choices transfer; the present seed-zero English suite cannot answer that question.
